from collections import Counter

def word_frequency_counter(words):
    stop_words = {"the", "and", "in", "of", "to", "a", "is", "it", "that", "for", "on", "was", "with", "as", "at", "by", "from", "are", "be", "this", "an", "or", "not", "you", "your", "all", "we", "can", "but", "has", "have", "if", "out", "so", "up", "what", "when", "which", "who", "will", "no", "there", "their", "then", "them", "these", "than", "how", "into", "only", "other", "its", "some", "do", "more", "any", "our", "over", "also", "new", "just", "like", "use", "could", "than", "where", "must", "should", "now"}

    word_count = Counter()
    for word in words:
        word = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\s]', '', word).lower()
        if word and word not in stop_words:
            word_count[word] += 1

    return dict(word_count)
```

The `word_frequency_counter` function first initializes a set of common stop words. It then uses a regular expression to remove non-alphanumeric characters and converts each word to lowercase. After that, it checks if the word is not empty and not a stop word before updating the word count using the `Counter` class. Finally, it returns the word frequency count as a dictionary.

